2025-05-01 00:46:04 - Thread-80 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and potential usage  
• "publication_number" (PUBLICATIONS): stores the patent/publication identifier we ultimately need to return.  
• "grant_date"            (PUBLICATIONS): numeric YYYYMMDD; filter to granted dates 20100101‑20181231.  
• "citation"              (PUBLICATIONS): VARIANT array of backward‑citation objects; must be flattened to read each object.  
  • f.value:"category" (after FLATTEN): field that (typically) holds the citation category such as SEA, NPL, X, etc.  
Other helpful filter columns  
• "application_kind"      (PUBLICATIONS): optional extra filter to granted patents (e.g. 'A', 'B', etc.).

Typical conditions  
• p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231  
• f.value:"category"::STRING = 'SEA'  

Ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 rows each)

```sql
-- 1) Quick look at publication numbers and grant dates
SELECT p."publication_number",
       p."grant_date"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Same, but restrict grants to 2010‑2018
SELECT p."publication_number",
       p."grant_date"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Distinct publication numbers granted 2010‑2018
SELECT DISTINCT p."publication_number"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Peek at the raw citation array for a few patents
SELECT p."publication_number",
       f.value AS "citation_entry"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN (input => p."citation") f
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Inspect the citation “category” field to learn its contents
SELECT p."publication_number",
       f.value:"category"::STRING AS "citation_category"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") f
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Return only SEA‑category citations (each row = one SEA citation)
SELECT p."publication_number"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") f
WHERE  p."grant_date"        BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
  AND  f.value:"category"::STRING = 'SEA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count how many SEA citations each patent has (no date filter yet)
SELECT p."publication_number",
       COUNT(*) AS "sea_citation_cnt"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") f
WHERE  f.value:"category"::STRING = 'SEA'
GROUP  BY p."publication_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Same count, but only for patents granted 2010‑2018
SELECT p."publication_number",
       COUNT(*) AS "sea_citation_cnt"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") f
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
  AND  f.value:"category"::STRING = 'SEA'
GROUP  BY p."publication_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Add HAVING to show patents that actually have at least one SEA citation
SELECT p."publication_number",
       COUNT(*) AS "sea_citation_cnt"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") f
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
  AND  f.value:"category"::STRING = 'SEA'
GROUP  BY p."publication_number"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10) Full answer pattern: publication number & SEA citation count,
--    ordered by most‑cited (still limited to sample rows)
SELECT p."publication_number",
       COUNT(*) AS "sea_citation_cnt"
FROM   PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") f
WHERE  p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20181231
  AND  f.value:"category"::STRING = 'SEA'
GROUP  BY p."publication_number"
ORDER  BY "sea_citation_cnt" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```
